// fleet domain -- every automation of the MemQL Cloud control plane (epic
// memql#3852).
//
// Three sections, three kinds of orchestration: the CONTROLLER that reconciles
// a cluster to its instance row (task memql#3853), BILLING, which turns events
// from two outside systems into graph writes (task memql#3854), and the TRIAL
// CLOCK and IDLE HIBERNATION, scheduled sweeps that act when a date passes
// (task memql#3856).
//
// ONE FILE, BECAUSE THE LOADER READS ONE FILE. The automation loader reads a
// domain's automations from its automations.memql and from no other file. The
// billing and trial automations used to live in billing.memql and trial.memql,
// where nothing ever loaded them: every other pass read those files and found
// them well formed, the bundle linted and booted clean, and no delivery was
// claimed, no dunning email staged, no trial paused and no suspended tenant
// torn down (memql#5437). An automation declared anywhere else is now refused
// at load (construct_misplaced), so the kinds are sections here rather than
// files.
//
// =============================================================================
//   The controller
// =============================================================================
//
// THE DESIGN IN ONE SENTENCE: the v1:fleet:instance row is DESIRED STATE, and
// these automations reconcile the world to it.
//
// That is the standard controller shape, and choosing it here bought three
// things that are otherwise fiddly:
//
//   1. NO CROSS-ROW LOOKUPS. Every automation below reads only the row that
//      triggered it. An instance row carries its slug, its domain, its profile,
//      its preset and its HA flag, so provisioning needs no join -- which
//      matters because an automation's actor is not the row's owner, and a
//      caller-scoped query from inside one would return nothing while looking
//      entirely correct.
//
//   2. LOOPS EXCLUDED BY TRIGGER FILTERS. The COMMAND vocabulary is the transient
//      statuses (`provisioning`, `suspending`, `resuming`, `tearing_down`).
//      Every action automation fires only on one of those and ends by writing a
//      SETTLED status (`running`, `suspended`, `torn_down`, `failed`) that no
//      action automation triggers on. A controller whose own writes cannot
//      re-trigger it needs no generation counter, no reconcile lock and no
//      debounce -- three things that are easy to add and hard to be sure of.
//      logic.memql states the corollary that constrains future changes.
//
//   3. AN HONEST PROGRESS SIGNAL FOR ORBIT. The console renders provisioning
//      progress by reading these transitions, and deliberately never reaches
//      ArgoCD from the browser: a console that queries the cluster's control
//      plane needs credentials for the cluster's control plane, which is a far
//      larger blast radius than "can read my own rows" for the sake of a
//      progress bar.
//
// WHY THE WELCOME EMAIL DOES NOT CARRY AN ENROLMENT LINK, which is a deliberate
// deviation from this task's acceptance criterion and an improvement on it. An
// enrolment token is a CREDENTIAL. Staging it in an outbound row would put a
// live credential in the graph at rest, in plaintext, in a concept whose own
// field documentation says "never secret material" -- and every other
// credential in this system is stored as a SHA-256 hash for exactly that
// reason. So the welcome email points at the tenant's own sign-in page and the
// credential ceremony happens there, through identity's magic-link or passkey
// path, which stores only hashes. The enrolment-token route stays available to
// an operator (scripts/install/enrolment-link.sh, memql#3408); what it does not
// do is travel through a graph row on the way to a mailbox.
//
// DELIVERY IS AT-LEAST-ONCE, so every automation here has to be idempotent
// against a redelivered event. Two properties carry that: the provisioning
// script is a pure function of its parameters (a replay rewrites byte-identical
// files and reports changed=false), and suspend / resume / teardown are
// naturally idempotent operations on a cluster. The status writes are
// last-write-wins onto the same row.

use fleet.actions.{ provisionTenant, resumeTenant, suspendTenant, tearDownTenant }
use fleet.logic.{ instanceCommand, runningInstances, suspendedInstances, teardownConfirmationPhrase, trialingSubscriptions }
use fleet.mutations.{ endSubscription, markInstanceRunning, markInstanceSuspended, markInstanceTornDown, requestInstanceSuspend, requestInstanceTeardown, setSubscriberStatus }
use platform.mutations.{ stageOutboundRequest, updateInboundRequestStatus }

// =============================================================================
//   Provisioning
// =============================================================================

/// A new tenant instance row exists: render its overlay and its ArgoCD Application, adopt it, and
/// mark it running. Fires on row CREATION, where the status is `provisioning` -- the settled
/// `pending` state exists for a row written ahead of any intent to act on it, and is not what
/// createInstance stamps.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "provisioning")
automation provisionInstanceOnCreate {
  args {
    id              any
    name            any
    domain          any
    instanceProfile any
    dbPreset        any
    haEnabled       any
    maxLlmCalls     any
    maxLlmCostUsd   any
    status          any
  }

  command := logic instanceCommand(status: args.status)
  switch command {
    case "provision" {
      action provisionTenant(tenant: args.name, domain: args.domain, profile: args.instanceProfile, dbPreset: args.dbPreset, ha: args.haEnabled, maxLlmCalls: args.maxLlmCalls, maxLlmCostUsd: args.maxLlmCostUsd, dryRun: false)
    }
    default { }
  }
  switch command {
    case "provision" {
      mutation markInstanceRunning(instanceId: args.id)
    }
    default { }
  }
}

/// An existing instance was moved back to `provisioning` -- a tier change, an HA add-on, or an
/// operator re-render. Runs the identical render, which is idempotent: unchanged inputs rewrite
/// byte-identical files and ArgoCD reconciles nothing.
///
/// A SEPARATE AUTOMATION FROM ITS CREATE TWIN rather than one with two triggers, because the two
/// events carry different meanings even where they run identical statements: this one is the
/// tier-change path, and the day it needs to differ -- draining connections before a downgrade, say
/// -- it is already its own construct rather than a branch inside a shared one.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "provisioning")
automation reRenderInstanceOnShapeChange {
  args {
    id              any
    name            any
    domain          any
    instanceProfile any
    dbPreset        any
    haEnabled       any
    maxLlmCalls     any
    maxLlmCostUsd   any
    status          any
  }

  command := logic instanceCommand(status: args.status)
  switch command {
    case "provision" {
      action provisionTenant(tenant: args.name, domain: args.domain, profile: args.instanceProfile, dbPreset: args.dbPreset, ha: args.haEnabled, maxLlmCalls: args.maxLlmCalls, maxLlmCostUsd: args.maxLlmCostUsd, dryRun: false)
    }
    default { }
  }
  switch command {
    case "provision" {
      mutation markInstanceRunning(instanceId: args.id)
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Suspend / resume
// =============================================================================

/// An instance was asked to suspend -- a trial that expired, a grace period that ran out, or an
/// idle hibernation. Scales it to zero and settles it as `suspended`, with every volume, backup and
/// namespace intact.
///
/// THE DATABASE STAYS UP (includeDatabase defaults false in the script). Scaling it down saves more,
/// but adds resume latency and takes the tenant's backups offline while it is down; task memql#3856
/// measures which one hibernation should default to.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "suspending")
automation suspendInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  command := logic instanceCommand(status: args.status)
  switch command {
    case "suspend" {
      action suspendTenant(tenant: args.name, dryRun: false)
    }
    default { }
  }
  switch command {
    case "suspend" {
      mutation markInstanceSuspended(instanceId: args.id)
    }
    default { }
  }
}

/// An instance was asked to resume -- a trial converted, a payment recovered, or a hibernating
/// tenant was touched. Brings it back up at the counts its overlay declares, INCLUDING a tier change
/// made while it was down, because the mesh is restored by handing sync back to ArgoCD rather than
/// by scaling to remembered numbers.
///
/// Resuming a torn-down instance is impossible and is not guarded here: `torn_down` is a settled
/// status, so nothing can move a torn-down row to `resuming` without going through a mutation that
/// would have to say so. That is why the two are different statuses rather than a flag on one.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "resuming")
automation resumeInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  command := logic instanceCommand(status: args.status)
  switch command {
    case "resume" {
      action resumeTenant(tenant: args.name, dryRun: false)
    }
    default { }
  }
  switch command {
    case "resume" {
      mutation markInstanceRunning(instanceId: args.id)
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Teardown
// =============================================================================

/// An instance was asked to tear down -- a grace period expired after cancellation, or a trial was
/// never converted. Takes a final backup and destroys the tenant. Irreversible.
///
/// THE CONFIRMATION PHRASE IS COMPOSED, NOT CONSTANT. tenant-teardown.sh demands
/// `teardown <tenant>`, and composing it per tenant is what preserves the property the phrase exists
/// for: a confirmation that names one tenant cannot authorise the destruction of another. An
/// automation passing a constant would have quietly removed that.
///
/// THE BACKUP FAILING ABORTS THE TEARDOWN. That is the script's behaviour, and the row is left in
/// `tearing_down` rather than settled -- a state that says what was being attempted, which is what
/// an operator needs and what a row that looked finished would deny them.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "tearing_down")
automation tearDownInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  command := logic instanceCommand(status: args.status)
  phrase := logic teardownConfirmationPhrase(tenant: args.name)
  switch command {
    case "teardown" {
      action tearDownTenant(tenant: args.name, confirm: phrase, dryRun: false)
    }
    default { }
  }
  switch command {
    case "teardown" {
      mutation markInstanceTornDown(instanceId: args.id)
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Onboarding
// =============================================================================

/// A subscriber's instance reached `running` for the first time: tell them where it is.
///
/// Staged as an outbound row rather than sent, which is the zero-Go delivery surface a product
/// bundle has (docs/public/operate/outbound-delivery.md) -- the engine's outbound worker drains it,
/// retries it, and records the outcome, none of which this bundle has to implement.
///
/// It fires on the `running` transition, which suspend and resume also produce, so a resumed tenant
/// gets this message again. That is deliberate for now and it is the honest trade: the alternative
/// is a "welcomed" flag on the instance row, which is state that exists only to suppress an email
/// and which drifts the first time a row is rebuilt. A second "your instance is ready" after a
/// resume is a mildly redundant email; a missed one is a customer who does not know their instance
/// exists. Task memql#3855 revisits it when Orbit owns the onboarding sequence.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance")
@filter(row => row.status == "running")
automation welcomeOnInstanceRunning {
  args {
    id          any
    name        any
    domain      any
    notifyEmail any
    status      any
  }

  switch args.status {
    case "running" {
      mutation stageOutboundRequest(
        requestId:   "fleet-welcome-" + args.id,
        medium:      "email",
        target:      args.notifyEmail,
        subject:     "Your MemQL instance is ready",
        body:        "Your MemQL instance is running at https://" + args.domain + " -- sign in there to get started.",
        requestedBy: "fleet.welcomeOnInstanceRunning"
      )
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   BILLING: Stripe in, meters out (task memql#3854)
// =============================================================================
//
// A different KIND of orchestration from the controller above, which
// reconciles a cluster to a desired-state row: this section translates events
// from two outside systems into graph writes. They share the domain and almost
// nothing else. These four lived in billing.memql until memql#5437, and none of
// them had ever loaded (see the head of this file).
//
// =============================================================================
//   Two inbound flows, one receiver
// =============================================================================
//
// Everything here rides `POST /inbound/{source}` -- the engine's
// signature-verifying webhook receiver -- and stages nothing of its own. No new
// HTTP endpoint, which was the epic's architecture decision and is now
// implementable: memql#3854 taught the receiver to read a COMPOSITE signature
// header, without which a Stripe delivery could not be verified at all (see
// component/inbound/verify_composite_test.go for what was actually wrong).
//
//   source `stripe`  -- Stripe's own webhooks. Money.
//   source `usage`   -- a TENANT reporting its own consumption. Metering.
//
// THE SECOND ONE IS THE INTERESTING DESIGN PROBLEM. Usage happens inside a
// tenant's cluster; the meters live on the mothership. There is no shared
// database between them and there must not be -- instance isolation is what a
// customer is buying. So a tenant REPORTS: it stages an outbound webhook row,
// the engine's outbound worker delivers it to the mothership's inbound
// receiver, and a handler here turns it into a `recordUsage` write. Both
// delivery halves already exist. The handler does NOT yet: turning a `usage`
// delivery into a meter needs its body read, and the DSL cannot read one -- the
// limit claimStripeDelivery below records -- so a `usage` delivery falls through
// claimStripeDelivery's default and is not counted.
//
// The consequence to hold onto: metering is EVENTUALLY consistent and
// at-least-once. A tenant that cannot reach the mothership keeps serving and
// reports later. That is deliberate -- the alternative is a customer's instance
// going down because our billing system is having a bad afternoon -- and it is
// exactly why the allowance ceiling is ALSO enforced inside the tenant, by
// configuration, where no network is involved. See the "two layers" note below.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Two layers of allowance, and why one of them is not enough
// =============================================================================
//
// LAYER 1, THE METER, is the accounting truth: what the customer consumed this
// period, what it costs, what Stripe is told. It is reconstructed from reports,
// so it lags, and a lost report under-bills by that report.
//
// LAYER 2, THE GUARD, is the structural backstop: the tier's ceiling rendered
// into the tenant's own `MEMQL_LLM_MAX_TOTAL_CALLS` / `_MAX_TOTAL_COST_USD`
// (docs/public/ai/llm-cost-control.md). It is enforced in-process, since boot,
// with no network and no graph read. A tenant cannot exceed it even if the
// mothership is unreachable, even if metering is broken, even if this file has
// a bug in it.
//
// Layer 2 is what makes the epic's launch gate -- "NO unbounded-spend path
// exists" -- a structural claim rather than a hope about our own correctness.
// A trial is the case that matters: its card has been authorized and never
// charged, so an unbounded trial is an unbounded gift. Which is why the trial
// tier's `overagePolicy` is `throttle` where every other tier meters, and why
// the tenant template renders a hard call cap for it.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Idempotency, which is not optional here
// =============================================================================
//
// The inbound receiver gives at-least-once delivery with a stable row id: a
// redelivery lands on the SAME v1:platform:inboundRequest row, but staging it
// is still a write, so the automation FIRES AGAIN. That is the documented
// contract (docs/public/operate/inbound-delivery.md) and it is the one thing a
// billing handler must not get wrong -- a doubled meter increment is a customer
// billed twice for one turn.
//
// The defence is the meter's identity rather than a dedupe check: one row per
// (subscription, period, metric), at a derived id, holding an ABSOLUTE total
// reported by the tenant. Re-applying the same report writes the same number.
// Reporting is idempotent because it is a SET, not an ADD -- which is why
// `recordUsage` takes totals and not deltas, and why the tenant is the one that
// counts.

// =============================================================================
//   Stripe -> the fleet graph
// =============================================================================

/// A verified inbound delivery arrived. If it came from Stripe, mark it as one nothing can handle
/// yet, with the reason on the row.
///
/// FIRES ON EVERY FRESHLY STAGED INBOUND ROW rather than filtering to `stripe` on the trigger. The
/// `switch` below is the source filter, and a delivery from any other source falls through its
/// default having done nothing -- which is a no-op rather than an error, because the set of sources
/// a deployment admits is configuration and is not knowable from here. `ingestCampaignFeedback` in
/// the campaigns domain has the same shape for the same reason.
///
/// THE STATUS FILTER IS WHAT STOPS IT CLAIMING ITS OWN WRITE. An update of the row publishes
/// node.created for its new version, so the `failed` stamp below is itself a delivery this trigger
/// sees; without `row.status == "received"` the automation re-triggers on it until the chain-depth
/// cap stops the run. The loop gate refused exactly that the first time this automation loaded
/// (memql#5437 -- it had lived in a file the loader never read). A redelivery restages the row as
/// `received`, so it is claimed again, which is the at-least-once contract the receiver documents.
///
/// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO is act on the event. It stamps `failed` with a reason and stops.
///
/// `failed`, NOT `processing`, and the difference matters more than it looks (corrected after
/// memql#3854 landed). `processing` means "a product automation has this in hand" -- so every Stripe
/// delivery would sit in that state forever, and an operator running
/// `inboundRequestsByStatus("processing")` would watch a pile grow that nothing was working. A
/// status that claims a worker which does not exist is worse than no handler at all: it is a
/// backlog that reads as progress.
///
/// `failed` is what the row IS. It is queryable, it carries `lastError` naming the missing piece,
/// and the day the JSON-extract builtin lands the same rows are re-drainable -- because the engine
/// never retries product-side handling itself, so nothing here is lost by saying so plainly.
///
/// The reason is worth stating precisely, because the obvious guess about it is wrong.
///
/// It is NOT the lookup. `subscriptionByStripeId` in queries.memql resolves a `sub_...` to a fleet
/// row perfectly well: @serverOnly + @unbounded is exactly the shape an internal sweep or webhook
/// handler takes, the same one `usersInDeletionCooldown` has carried in the identity domain for
/// some time, and a logic reaches it with OriginInternal stamped by the step executor.
///
/// It is that THE DSL CANNOT READ THE EVENT. `v1:platform:inboundRequest.body` is the verified raw
/// request body as a STRING, and the automation evaluator has no JSON parsing -- no field
/// extraction, no path read, nothing that turns those bytes into `data.object.subscription`. So a
/// handler can see that a Stripe delivery arrived and cannot see what it says.
///
/// That is a real limit on the inbound surface's "no product Go" promise, and this is the second
/// thing to hit it: campaigns needed a Go builtin (`integration.campaigns.ingestFeedback`) for the
/// identical reason -- it parses a provider payload off a staged row because nothing in the DSL
/// could. The fix is generic and belongs in the engine rather than here: one JSON-extract builtin
/// would serve every product draining `POST /inbound/{source}`, which is the entire point of that
/// surface existing. Recorded on memql#3854.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:platform:inboundRequest")
@filter(row => row.status == "received")
automation claimStripeDelivery {
  args {
    id     any
    source any
    status any
  }

  switch args.source {
    case "stripe" {
      mutation updateInboundRequestStatus(
        requestId: args.id,
        status:    "failed",
        lastError: "no handler: the DSL cannot parse a webhook body, so the Stripe event cannot be mapped to a fleet row (memql#3854). Re-drainable once a JSON-extract builtin lands."
      )
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Dunning -- a payment failed, and what happens next
// =============================================================================
//
// THE POLICY, stated once: a failed payment starts a CLOCK, it does not cut
// service. Seven days of grace, an email at the start of it, suspension at the
// end, and an immediate resume on recovery.
//
// WHAT RUNS is the start of that clock and the recovery flag: the account goes
// `delinquent` and the email is staged, and it returns to `active` when the
// subscription does. The END of the clock is not automated -- no sweep reads a
// past_due subscription's graceEndsAt and suspends its instance -- and neither
// is the resume; both are an operator's act today.
//
// Cutting a customer off the moment a card expires loses the customer AND the
// invoice -- the two things dunning exists to protect. Most failed payments are
// an expired card and a person who has not read their email yet, not a refusal
// to pay. The subscriber row goes `delinquent` so the state is visible in the
// fleet console and in Orbit; the instance keeps running.

/// A subscription entered `past_due`: mark the account delinquent (the email is
/// dunningEmailOnPaymentFailure's). The instance is untouched -- see the policy note above.
/// `graceEndsAt` was set by whoever wrote `past_due` -- an operator today, the Stripe path once it
/// can read a delivery -- and no sweep acts on it yet.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:subscription")
automation dunningNoticeOnPaymentFailure {
  args {
    id           any
    subscriberId any
    status       any
  }

  switch args.status {
    case "past_due" {
      mutation setSubscriberStatus(
        subscriberId: args.subscriberId,
        status:       "delinquent"
      )
    }
    default { }
  }
}

/// A subscription recovered: clear the account's delinquency. `activateSubscription` already
/// cleared the grace clock -- a subscription that is active is not in any grace period by
/// definition, and a stale `graceEndsAt` is how a recovered account gets torn down a week later.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:subscription")
automation clearDelinquencyOnRecovery {
  args {
    id           any
    subscriberId any
    status       any
  }

  switch args.status {
    case "active" {
      mutation setSubscriberStatus(
        subscriberId: args.subscriberId,
        status:       "active"
      )
    }
    default { }
  }
}

/// A payment failed: tell the person who pays.
///
/// FIRES ON THE SUBSCRIPTION, not on the subscriber, even though `billingEmail` is conceptually the
/// account's. Two reasons, and the second is the one that decided it:
///
///   Every automation in this domain reads only the row that triggered it, because an automation's
///   actor is not the row's owner and a caller-scoped read from inside one returns nothing while
///   looking entirely correct. So the address is denormalised onto the subscription, exactly as
///   `instance.notifyEmail` is.
///
///   And the IDEMPOTENCY KEY has to change per dunning CYCLE. `stageOutboundRequest` is idempotent
///   by requestId -- a re-stage onto an existing row preserves the worker-owned delivery state
///   rather than resending -- which is exactly right for the at-least-once event stream this fires
///   on, and exactly wrong if the key is constant per account: the customer's SECOND late payment,
///   months later, would land on a row already marked `sent` and no mail would go out. Keying on
///   `graceEndsAt` makes it once per cycle, which is what "tell them their payment failed" means.
///
/// The message names no amount and no card. It cannot: this row does not carry them, Stripe does,
/// and a dunning email that guesses at a figure is worse than one that does not mention it.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:subscription")
automation dunningEmailOnPaymentFailure {
  args {
    id           any
    billingEmail any
    graceEndsAt  any
    status       any
  }

  switch args.status {
    case "past_due" {
      mutation stageOutboundRequest(
        requestId:   "fleet-dunning-" + args.id + "-" + args.graceEndsAt,
        medium:      "email",
        target:      args.billingEmail,
        subject:     "Action needed: your MemQL Cloud payment did not go through",
        body:        "We could not process your latest MemQL Cloud payment. Your instance keeps running -- please update your payment details in the billing portal within the grace period to avoid interruption.",
        requestedBy: "fleet.dunningEmailOnPaymentFailure"
      )
    }
    default { }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   The TRIAL CLOCK and IDLE HIBERNATION (task memql#3856)
// =============================================================================
//
// Six scheduled sweeps. Five of them are the entire lifecycle of a customer who
// never pays: nudged at day 7 and day 12, suspended at day 14, offered their
// data back for 14 more, and then torn down. The sixth hibernates an idle
// instance of any tier. They lived in trial.memql until memql#5437, and none of
// them had ever loaded (see the head of this file).
//
// =============================================================================
//   Why these are sweeps at all, and how they are allowed to read
// =============================================================================
//
// Everything else in this domain reacts to a row that changed. A clock cannot:
// nothing writes a row when a date passes. So these six are `@trigger(schedule=)`
// and they READ -- which is the one thing this domain's automations otherwise
// never do, because an automation's actor is not the row's owner and a
// caller-scoped read from inside one returns nothing while looking correct.
//
// What makes it legal is the pair of annotations on the queries they call
// (queries.memql, "Internal sweeps"): `@serverOnly` refuses a client-originated
// call outright, and `@unbounded` declares that reading every row is the intent
// -- with the reason recorded, so the pagination gate does not silently truncate
// a sweep to its first page and leave the tail of the fleet unswept. That is the
// same shape `usersInDeletionCooldown` has carried in the identity domain, and
// deploy/fleet/authz_test.go checks BOTH annotations are present, because either
// alone is a defect wearing the other's clothes.
//
// =============================================================================
//   Every sweep reads exactly ONE concept, and that is why the clocks are
//   denormalised
// =============================================================================
//
// A sweep cannot join. So the clock a sweep reads has to be on the row it
// sweeps: `instance.trialEndsAt` is copied from the subscription, and
// `instance.suspendedAt` is the grace clock on the DATA rather than on the
// billing relationship.
//
// That is not duplication for convenience -- it is what makes the sweeps
// possible at all, and it is the same reason `notifyEmail`, `billingEmail`, the
// two spend ceilings and the shape fields are denormalised. The cost is that a
// clock moved on the subscription must be re-stamped on the instance; the
// benefit is that no sweep in this section can be silently returning nothing.
//
// =============================================================================
//   The windowing lives in the loop, not in the filter
// =============================================================================
//
// "trialEndsAt is between 7 and 8 days away" is computed per row in the `for`
// loop's `if` clause, over the candidate rows runningInstances returns. The
// bracket is one day wide and the sweep fires daily, which is what makes each
// nudge fire EXACTLY ONCE per instance -- no duplicates from a wide bracket, no
// gaps from a narrow one.
//
// =============================================================================
//   What a trial costs, and what bounds it
// =============================================================================
//
// A trial runs Graph-level FEATURES on Node-shaped infrastructure (the `solo`
// profile, `entry` database) with the tier's hard cap: 500 message credits,
// `overagePolicy: throttle`.
//
// The caps are enforced in the tenant's own process by the spend ceiling
// (task memql#3854), not by these sweeps. That distinction matters: a sweep runs
// once a day, and a runaway does not wait for it. What these do is bound the
// trial in TIME; what bounds it in SPEND is a limit on the other side of the
// boundary that needs no network and no clock.

// =============================================================================
//   Day 7 and day 12 -- the nudges
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 09:00 UTC: tell every trial that is a week from ending. The first of two nudges; the second
/// is two days out (below), which is when somebody who meant to buy actually acts.
///
/// The email is keyed on the instance AND the milestone, so the two nudges are different outbound
/// rows and neither suppresses the other -- `stageOutboundRequest` is idempotent by requestId, so a
/// shared key would mean the day-12 message silently never sent.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 9 * * *")
automation trialNudgeDay7 {
  decide := logic runningInstances(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.tier == "trial" && item.trialEndsAt > addDuration(now, "P7D") && item.trialEndsAt <= addDuration(now, "P8D") {
    mutation stageOutboundRequest(
      requestId:   "fleet-trial-nudge-7-" + item.id,
      medium:      "email",
      target:      item.notifyEmail,
      subject:     "One week left on your MemQL Cloud trial",
      body:        "Your trial instance at https://" + item.domain + " has a week to run. Add a plan in Orbit to keep it -- nothing is lost if you decide later, but the instance pauses when the trial ends.",
      requestedBy: "fleet.trialNudgeDay7"
    )
  }
}

/// Daily 09:00 UTC: the two-days-out nudge. Deliberately a different message from the day-7 one --
/// this is the last one before the instance pauses, and it says what happens next rather than
/// repeating the invitation.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 9 * * *")
automation trialNudgeDay12 {
  decide := logic runningInstances(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.tier == "trial" && item.trialEndsAt > addDuration(now, "P2D") && item.trialEndsAt <= addDuration(now, "P3D") {
    mutation stageOutboundRequest(
      requestId:   "fleet-trial-nudge-12-" + item.id,
      medium:      "email",
      target:      item.notifyEmail,
      subject:     "Your MemQL Cloud trial ends in two days",
      body:        "Your trial instance at https://" + item.domain + " pauses in two days. Your data stays exactly where it is for 14 days after that, and adding a plan in Orbit brings it straight back.",
      requestedBy: "fleet.trialNudgeDay12"
    )
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Day 14 -- the trial ends
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 02:00 UTC: pause every trial instance whose clock has run out. Scale-to-zero, data intact
/// -- which is the whole shape of the offer: a trial that ends does not destroy anything, it stops
/// costing us compute.
///
/// The instance and the subscription are closed by two separate sweeps, because neither can read the
/// other. They are eventually consistent with each other, and the order does not matter: a paused
/// instance whose subscription is still `trialing` for a few hours is a customer who cannot use a
/// service they are not paying for, which is the intended state either way.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 2 * * *")
automation trialExpirySuspend {
  decide := logic runningInstances(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.tier == "trial" && item.trialEndsAt != nil && item.trialEndsAt < now {
    mutation requestInstanceSuspend(instanceId: item.id)
  }
}

/// Daily 02:00 UTC: close out every subscription whose trial has ended, and start the 14-day clock
/// on the data.
///
/// `paused`, not `cancelled`. The customer did not cancel -- they ran out of trial, which is a
/// different fact about the same person and the one that decides whether "convert" or "resubscribe"
/// is the right word in every message that follows.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 2 * * *")
automation trialExpiryClose {
  decide := logic trialingSubscriptions(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.trialEndsAt != nil && item.trialEndsAt < now {
    mutation endSubscription(
      subscriptionId: item.id,
      status:         "paused",
      graceEndsAt:    addDuration(now, "P14D")
    )
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Hibernation -- an instance nobody is using
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 03:00 UTC: scale to zero any instance that has served nothing for 14 days.
///
/// THIS APPLIES TO EVERY TIER, not just trials, and that is deliberate. A paying customer's idle
/// instance costs us exactly as much as an idle trial's, and resuming is a scale-up rather than a
/// restore -- the data never moved. What a paid tier buys is not "always warm", it is capacity when
/// they use it; a tier that genuinely needs zero cold starts is a product decision with a price
/// attached, not a default.
///
/// `lastActivityAt` is stamped by the TENANT's own heartbeat, because the control plane cannot see
/// the tenant's traffic. An instance that has never reported activity therefore has an empty field
/// and is skipped rather than hibernated -- fail-safe in the direction that costs money rather than
/// the one that surprises a customer.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 3 * * *")
automation hibernateIdleInstances {
  decide := logic runningInstances(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.lastActivityAt != nil && addDuration(item.lastActivityAt, "P14D") < now {
    mutation requestInstanceSuspend(instanceId: item.id)
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   The end of the grace period
// =============================================================================

/// Daily 04:00 UTC: tear down instances that have been suspended for 14 days.
///
/// This is the only automation in the fleet that destroys data, and it is a SWEEP rather than a
/// reaction for exactly that reason: nothing a customer or an operator does in one action reaches
/// it. The path is pause, then fourteen days, then this.
///
/// The capability script takes a final backup before deleting anything and ABORTS if that backup
/// fails, so a teardown that could not preserve the data does not happen. The instance is left in
/// `tearing_down` in that case -- a state that says what was being attempted, which is what an
/// operator needs and what a row that looked finished would deny them.
///
/// `suspendedAt` is the clock, and it is the instance's OWN field rather than the subscription's
/// `graceEndsAt`. The two normally agree; where they do not, this one is the honest one -- it
/// measures how long the data has actually been cold, which is the thing the grace period is about.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 4 * * *")
automation teardownAfterGrace {
  decide := logic suspendedInstances(event: event)
  for item in decide if item.suspendedAt != nil && addDuration(item.suspendedAt, "P14D") < now {
    mutation requestInstanceTeardown(instanceId: item.id)
  }
}
